('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'trig-for-side-lengths', 5, 'Easy',
 'A right triangle has an opposite side of 6 and an angle of 45°. What is the length of the adjacent side?', 3,
 '[{"text": "8.5", "feedback": "This looks like it multiplied by √2 instead of dividing by tan(45°) — recompute 6 ÷ tan(45°)."}, {"text": "4.2", "feedback": "This looks like it used sine or cosine instead of tangent — the relationship between opposite and adjacent uses tangent."}, {"text": "12", "feedback": "This doubles the opposite side rather than dividing by tan(45°), which equals 1."}, {"text": "6", "feedback": "Correct. adjacent = opposite ÷ tan(45°) = 6 ÷ 1 = 6, since tan(45°) = 1."}]'::jsonb,
 'solving-right-triangle-side'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'trig-for-side-lengths', 6, 'Easy',
 'A person on top of a cliff looks down at a boat. The angle between their horizontal line of sight and their line of sight to the boat is called the angle of ___.', 1,
 '[{"text": "Elevation", "feedback": "Elevation is measured looking upward from horizontal, such as from the boat looking up at the person — here the person is looking down."}, {"text": "Depression", "feedback": "Correct. Looking downward from horizontal to a lower point is the angle of depression."}, {"text": "Reflection", "feedback": "\"Angle of reflection\" is a physics term used for light or waves bouncing off a surface, not for a line of sight to an object."}, {"text": "Inclination", "feedback": "\"Angle of inclination\" isn''t the standard term used in this situation — it''s called the angle of depression."}]'::jsonb,
 'elevation-vs-depression-terminology'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'trig-for-side-lengths', 7, 'Easy',
 'The angle of elevation from point A to point B, and the angle of depression from point B to point A, are always related how?', 0,
 '[{"text": "They are equal", "feedback": "Correct. These two angles are alternate angles formed by a transversal (the line of sight) between two parallel horizontal lines, so they''re always equal."}, {"text": "They add to 90°", "feedback": "90° would make them complementary, but that''s not the relationship here — they''re actually equal to each other."}, {"text": "They add to 180°", "feedback": "180° would make them supplementary, but that''s not the relationship here — they''re actually equal to each other."}, {"text": "There''s no fixed relationship between them", "feedback": "These two angles are alternate angles formed between parallel horizontal lines, which are always exactly equal."}]'::jsonb,
 'elevation-depression-alternate-angles-relationship'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'trig-for-side-lengths', 8, 'Easy',
 'As you walk closer to the base of a tall building (staying the same height), does the angle of elevation to its top increase or decrease?', 0,
 '[{"text": "It increases", "feedback": "Correct. As the horizontal distance shrinks while the building''s height stays the same, the line of sight gets steeper, increasing the angle of elevation."}, {"text": "It decreases", "feedback": "Getting closer to a tall object makes you tilt your head back further to see the top, which means the angle gets larger, not smaller."}, {"text": "It stays the same", "feedback": "The angle of elevation depends on the ratio of height to horizontal distance — since the distance is changing, the angle changes too."}, {"text": "It becomes an angle of depression instead", "feedback": "Depression only applies when looking downward from a higher point — here, the observer stays at ground level."}]'::jsonb,
 'elevation-angle-distance-relationship'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'trig-for-side-lengths', 9, 'Easy',
 'You stand on the ground and look up at a kite in the sky. From what reference line is this angle measured?', 2,
 '[{"text": "The vertical line straight up", "feedback": "The angle is measured from the horizontal, not from a vertical reference line."}, {"text": "The line to the kite''s shadow", "feedback": "The kite''s shadow isn''t part of how the angle of elevation is defined — it''s measured from a horizontal line of sight."}, {"text": "The horizontal", "feedback": "Correct. The angle of elevation is always measured from a horizontal line of sight upward to the object."}, {"text": "The ground directly below the kite", "feedback": "The reference line for an angle of elevation is horizontal, in the direction the observer is facing — not a vertical line to a point on the ground."}]'::jsonb,
