('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'trig-for-side-lengths', 36, 'Advanced',
 'Two people stand on opposite sides of a tower, 100 m apart in total. Their angles of elevation to the top are 35° and 50°. Find the tower''s height, to one decimal place.', 1,
 '[{"text": "35.7 m", "feedback": "This doesn''t satisfy h/tan(35°) + h/tan(50°) = 100 — recompute using both angles together, since the two people are on opposite sides."}, {"text": "44.1 m", "feedback": "Correct. Since the two people are on opposite sides, their distances from the tower (h/tan(35°) and h/tan(50°)) add up to 100 m: h(1/tan(35°) + 1/tan(50°)) = 100, giving h ≈ 44.1 m."}, {"text": "50.0 m", "feedback": "This doesn''t come from solving the combined equation — set up h/tan(35°) + h/tan(50°) = 100 and solve for h."}, {"text": "83.9 m", "feedback": "This is too large — recheck the equation h(1/tan(35°) + 1/tan(50°)) = 100 for h."}]'::jsonb,
 'elevation-opposite-sides-problem'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'trig-for-side-lengths', 37, 'Advanced',
 'A plane flying at an altitude of 3000 m has an angle of depression of 12° to an airport. How far (along the ground) is the plane from the airport, to the nearest metre?', 2,
 '[{"text": "637 m", "feedback": "This looks like it used tan(12°) × 3000 instead of dividing — since 3000 m is the opposite side and the ground distance is adjacent, divide by tan(12°) instead."}, {"text": "3000 m", "feedback": "This just reuses the altitude — the ground distance is a separate value, found using the tangent ratio."}, {"text": "14 114 m", "feedback": "Correct. tan(12°) = 3000 ÷ d, so d = 3000 ÷ tan(12°) ≈ 14 114 m."}, {"text": "3068 m", "feedback": "This looks like it used cosine instead of tangent — with the altitude as the opposite side and the ground distance as adjacent, tangent is the correct ratio."}]'::jsonb,
 'elevation-depression-large-scale'),
('MPM2D', 'trigonometry', 'trig-for-side-lengths', 38, 'Advanced',
 'A surveyor on top of an 80 m hill sees two objects in the same direction, at angles of depression 10° and 35°. Find the distance between the two objects, to one decimal place.', 3,
 '[{"text": "453.7 m", "feedback": "This adds the two individual distances instead of subtracting them — since both objects are in the same direction, the gap between them is a difference, not a sum."}, {"text": "113.9 m", "feedback": "This is only the closer object''s distance from the hill''s base — the farther object''s distance still needs to be subtracted from it."}, {"text": "341.1 m", "feedback": "Recompute both distances (80÷tan(10°) and 80÷tan(35°)) individually before finding their difference."}, {"text": "339.5 m", "feedback": "Correct. The two objects'' distances from the hill''s base are 80÷tan(10°) ≈ 453.7 m and 80÷tan(35°) ≈ 114.2 m; the distance between them is the difference, about 339.5 m."}]'::jsonb,
